WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.300
Three classic thresholds help design waiting:

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.200
0.1 seconds feels immediate, 1 second preserves flow

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.600
and 10 seconds marks the approximate limit of continuous attention.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.700
These are interaction references, not universal laws.

5
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.700
Above one second, the interface should show that work continues;

6
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.400
above ten, it needs visible progress and a clear way to interrupt.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:32.600
TTFT measures from sending the request until the first visible token appears.

8
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.200
Total latency ends when the last token arrives.

9
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:41.700
Two systems with the same total time can feel different if one starts responding sooner.

10
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.700
Streaming reduces silent waiting; it does not remove the remaining work.

11
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:55.100
Before the first token, work can happen that the user cannot see.

12
00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.100
After that, streaming delivers the answer progressively while generation continues.

13
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.600
The improvement is perceptual: the user gets a sign of life earlier.

14
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.400
Total time can still remain unchanged.

15
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.500
RouteLLM learns when to use a stronger model and when to use a weaker one to balance quality and cost.

16
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.500
In its evaluation, some routers reduced cost by more than two times without degrading measured quality.

17
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.000
In a product, that separation can map fast routes to simple queries and reserve more capacity for difficult ones.

18
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.400
Latency policy remains a system decision.

19
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.600
The robust pattern is not to always think longer,

20
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.800
but to classify the request, assign a budget and show progress before waiting looks like failure.

21
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.300
When the budget is exhausted, the system needs an explicit fallback:

22
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.700
partial answer, clarification, alternate model or recoverable error.

23
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.600
Knowing when to stop is part of the design.
